Subject: Cache the global name resolution of OP_get_var

What this does
  Adds a per-context, per-runtime-validated cache in front of the global name
  resolution performed by OP_get_var, OP_get_var_undef and the OP_put_var
  handlers' read side.

Why we need it
  Resolving one global name probes two objects and walks their hash chains --
  ctx->global_var_obj for a global lexical binding, then ctx->global_obj for a
  `var` or function declaration -- and publishes the current pc through
  sf->cur_pc before calling out, on every execution of the opcode.

What it adds
  A 128-set, 2-way table keyed on the low bits of the atom, memoizing "this atom
  is own data property #offset of this object". A hit returns a new reference to
  the same slot the probe would have found. Coherence is by generation, not by
  assumption: a single counter is bumped by add_property(), add_shape_property()
  and js_shape_prepare_update(), which between them gate every change to any
  watched object's property layout, and a cell is only filled on a path that has
  already established the property is a data property and, for the lexical arm,
  has already passed the temporal-dead-zone test -- the sole writer of
  JS_UNINITIALIZED into global_var_obj is JS_DefineGlobalVar immediately after
  its own add_property(). The two global objects carry a bit marking them
  watched, so a realm that does not own them still invalidates on their behalf.
  No opcode is added or retired and BC_VERSION is unchanged, so the built-in
  blobs are not regenerated.

Needs a bytecode version bump
  No.
